eliezer (0.4.0) UNRELEASED; urgency=low

  * Add the embedded llama.cpp runtime (features rocm, vulkan, cuda and cpu; without one the
    service cannot load models). It serves chat with streaming, parallel sequences in one batch,
    reuse of a shared prompt prefix, stop sequences, JSON-schema output and embeddings. Checked on
    a Radeon RX 7900 XTX with Mistral 7B, olmOCR 2 7B, gpt-oss 20B, Gemma 4 26B, Qwen3.8 27B and
    Qwen3 Coder 30B: loading takes 2 to 10 s, 109 to 166 tokens/s (Qwen3.8: 36), and unloading
    returns the model memory (a constant 0.2 to 0.45 GB of ROCm runtime stays, it does not grow).
  * Render the chat template of each model (Jinja, from the GGUF) and parse its output into text,
    reasoning and tool calls: Qwen XML, Harmony (gpt-oss), Gemma 4 and think tags. Stop sequences
    apply to the visible answer only, and the end token of a Harmony tool call reaches the parser.
  * Add `eliezer --import DIR` with `server.run_user` and `server.run_group`: move GGUF files into
    the model store, hand them to the service account and enter the language and embedding models
    into the configuration. The result is validated like a service start, written atomically with a
    backup, and a broken model list does not block the import. Image models are moved and only
    noted, vision projectors get no entry, and entries that point at the wrong kind of file are
    reported. Options: --copy, --dry-run, --no-config and --suggest FILE.
  * Read the memory layout from the GGUF header, including per-layer head counts stored as int32,
    sliding-window patterns (Gemma 4) and linear-attention layers with their state (Qwen3.8). The
    estimate of these models was two to three times too high, so the planner refused them (HTTP
    409).
  * Add API key management: `eliezer --new-key [--name N] [--save]` creates an OpenAI-style key
    (sk- and 256 random bits), enters the key file in the configuration, shows the key once and
    can save a copy in ~/.config/eliezer/api_key; `--list-keys` shows the keys masked and whether
    a Hugging Face token is set; `--revoke-key NAME` removes one; `--set-hf-token` stores the
    token from standard input, ~/.config/eliezer/hf_key, the default Hugging Face token file or a
    hidden prompt. The service compares
    keys in constant time and answers a wrong or missing key with 401 and code invalid_api_key.
  * Add a command line client to the eliezer binary (ls, ps, status, show, load, unload, run, help)
    in the style of ollama and lms, with --host, --key-file, --json, ELIEZER_TIMEOUT_SECS and exit
    codes 0 to 3. It names the systemd state when nothing answers, says when a server accepts but
    does not answer, and warns when the VRAM budget is far below the card.
  * Send a Server header on every response; the client refuses other programs on the port.
  * Report a model file that was moved or deleted: warnings in --check-config, at start and on
    reload, and HTTP 502 model_file instead of a generic backend error when it is loaded. Name the
    section of a misplaced configuration field, and the address and reason when binding fails.
  * Fix the systemd unit for a state directory or model store that is a symbolic link, a mount or
    under /home: drop StateDirectory= (systemd refuses a link there), create /var/lib/eliezer in the
    package, and make /home read-only instead of hidden. A drop-in example is installed.
  * Build the Debian package with cargo-deb, one variant per GPU API (a plain build contains the
    ROCm runtime), with the ROCm libraries found through an rpath. Check install, start, reload,
    reinstall, removal and a symlinked state directory in a clean container (tests/deb).
  * Add manual GPU checks (tests/m4: live-check.py, vram-cycles.py, layouts.py) and record Burn and
    Candle (AMD support) under tests/runtime.

 -- Jörg Bernau <Joerg.Bernau@web.de>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 21:30:44 +0200

eliezer (0.3.0) UNRELEASED; urgency=low

  * Calculate the VRAM requirement per architecture type and schedule loading, eviction,
    pinning, batching and idle handling via a state machine with a clock trait.
  * Measure GPU usage uniformly via a trait (NVML, sysfs, nvidia-smi, rocm-smi) with vendor
    detection, timeout and fallback to bookkeeping.
  * Add the backend trait, a fake backend and the supervisor task.
  * Provide the management API, the OpenAI and Anthropic interfaces with unbuffered SSE,
    authentication and a uniform error format, plus /v1/completions, /v1/embeddings and
    /v1/images/generations.
  * Report queue, queue length and last use per model in /api/v1/status.
  * Apply a changed configuration and key file on SIGHUP: add and remove models, unload removed
    ones when idle, keep loaded models.
  * Integrate the supervisor, the key file (0600) and the GPU measurement into the service program.
  * Verify the model list in a contract test against a real LM Studio response.
  * Test the documented cases over HTTP, including chat/image alternation, request cancellation,
    GPU measurement failures and an end-to-end configuration reload.
  * Write the documentation, messages and comments in English and give the documents English names.

